with strong recurrent baselines on training loss and multiple-choice accuracy, while containment-style tasks remain more challenging. At 410M, the SiLU variant improves the multiple-choice average over the external baselines; at 1.45B, the inner-product linear variant gives the strongest Modular TTT result and is close to GDN on average multiple-choice accuracy. AppendixD.5reports additional external-baseline checks, including the LaCT comparison.\nPer-token Loss.AppendixD.6further examines per-token loss along 8k sequences, showing that the selected shallow Modular TTT variants remain stable around and beyond the 4k training context.\nRULER.Detailed RULER [ 21] evaluations are reported in AppendixD.7, with family-level and template-level results in Tables31–32. The inner-product linear variant is the strongest Modular TTT configuration on niah_single at 410M, while the MSE SiLU variant is strongest at 1.45B for contexts up to 4k. However, LLaMA remains substantially stronger, especially at 8k, indicating that precise long-context recall remains a limitation of the current shallow Modular TTT variants.\n11\n\n\n Table 9Large-scale downstream evaluation at 410M and 1.45B. Arrows indicate the direction of improvement. External baselines: LLaMA [48], GDN [52], and LaCT [54] where available.\nMethod Params Perplexity Multiple-choice Containment\nloss↓\nWiki. ppl↓\nLMB. ppl↓\nAvg ppl↓\nBoolQ acc↑\nPIQA acc↑\nHella. acc-n↑\nWino. acc↑\nARC-e acc↑\nARC-c acc-n↑\nOBQA acc-n↑\nSIQA acc↑\nAvg acc↑\nSWDE acc↑\nSQuAD acc↑\nFDA acc↑\nAvg acc↑\nExternal baselines, 410M\nLLaMA 410M 2.547920.63 25.64 23.14 59.51 69.15 47.47 55.80 65.28 31.83 35.40 40.63 50.63 58.96 40.42 62.43 53.94 GDN 410M 2.555921.00 22.62 21.81 59.69 71.00 47.64 52.88 65.66 31.83 34.80 40.07 50.45 22.68 31.80 16.33 23.60 LaCT 410M 2.558221.28 25.94 23.61 60.86 70.13 47.10 52.49 65.03 30.03 35.80 40.02 50.18 33.75 30.16 18.60 27.50\nModular TTT MSE Loss, 410M\nM 410M 2.564821.28 25.10 23.19 53.76 69.04 47.92 52.